course: dictionary
lesson: 13561
title: Inaccuracy: when something is not exact
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An inaccuracy is a mistake, or a wrong fact, in something said, written or measured. It also means that something is not exact or correct. The word comes from inaccurate, and the ending -cy turns it into a noun.
explain: «The news report had three inaccuracies about the football match.» Each inaccuracy is a wrong fact, so the report did not match what really happened. Ask yourself: what is wrong here, and what would the correct fact be?
explain: Inaccuracy is also used in science. «The ruler was bent, so the length had some inaccuracy.» The amount by which a measurement is wrong is its inaccuracy. Scientists repeat measurements to make that amount smaller.
fact: An inaccuracy is a mistake or a wrong fact.
fact: Inaccuracy is a noun. Its plural is inaccuracies.
fact: «Full of inaccuracies» means containing many mistakes.
fact: In measurement, inaccuracy is the amount by which a measurement is wrong.
fact: Repeating a measurement and checking your tools can reduce inaccuracy.
exercise: «The science report had an inaccuracy in the final answer.» What is an inaccuracy? | A very fast race | A kind of measuring cup | *A mistake or wrong fact | A long, careful drawing
exercise: «The class repeated the measurement to reduce the inaccuracy.» Why did they repeat it? | To make the ruler longer | *To check and make the mistake smaller | To make the answer bigger | To stop measuring
exercise: Which word is the plural of inaccuracy? | *inaccuracies | inaccurate | accurate | inaccurately
test: «The history book was full of inaccuracies about the king.» What does full of inaccuracies mean? | Full of praise | Full of pictures | *Full of mistakes | Full of empty pages
test: A measurement is off by 2 grams. Which word means how far the measurement is wrong? | Length | *Inaccuracy | Accuracy | Weight
exam: «Ella checked the news story and found three inaccuracies.» What does three inaccuracies mean? | Three happy readers | Three correct facts | Three pages | *Three wrong facts or mistakes
